  <a href="#section-debug-mode" id="section-debug-mode" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Logging emails instead of sending</h2>

  <section>
	  <div class="view">
		  <p>
			  You can configure the <code>Mailer</code> to log emails instead of sending them.
		  </p>
		  <p>
			  Note: normally emails are always logged on <code>Level.DEBUG</code> level, but in logging mode emails are logged in <code>Level.INFO</code>.
		  </p>
	  </div>

	  <div class="side">
<pre><code>Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withTransportModeLoggingOnly()
    .(...)
    .buildMailer();

mailer.sync().sendMail(email); // <-- this email will be logged but not sent
</code></pre>
		  or:
		  <pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.transport.mode.logging.only=true
</code></pre>
	  </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-override-receivers" id="section-override-receivers" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Override envelope-level receivers</h2>

  <section>
	  <div class="view">
		  <p>
			  For testing purposes, you can change where the email is sent to, without touching the actual email being sent, by overriding the envelope-level
			  receivers. This keeps test routing outside the message content while preserving the original addresses for inspection.
		  </p>
	  </div>

	  <div class="side">
<pre><code class="small">// send the email to your testers
currentEmailBuilder.withOverrideReceivers(tester1Recipient, testGroupInboxRecipient);</code></pre>
	  </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-debug-logging" id="section-debug-logging" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Enable Jakarta Mail debug logging</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        By default Jakarta Mail's debug logging is turned off, but you can turn it on for more diagnostic info. You can also route the debug stream
        to stdout, stderr or SLF4J without touching the internal Session instance directly.
      </p>
	  <p>
		  Use <code class="inline language-java">withDebugPrinter(PrintStream)</code> when the built-in targets do not fit. Simple Java Mail passes that
		  stream to Jakarta Mail but does not close it. Keep it open for as long as the Mailer may use it, then close it yourself.
	  </p>
	  <p>
		  A custom <code class="inline language-java">PrintStream</code> is a Java-only setting: a live stream cannot be represented by a property or CLI
		  argument. For those forms, choose <code class="inline language-java">STDOUT</code>, <code class="inline language-java">STDERR</code>, or
		  <code class="inline language-java">SLF4J</code> through <code class="inline language-java">SessionDebugOutput</code> instead.
	  </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
Mailer mailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("host", 587, "username", "password")
    .withDebugLogging(true)
    .withDebugOutput(SessionDebugOutput.SLF4J)
    .buildMailer();

// or
mailer.getSession().setDebug(true);

// or
yourSession.setDebug(true);
mail.mailerBuilder(yourSession);
</code></pre>
      or:
      <pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug=true
simplejavamail.javaxmail.debug.out=SLF4J
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
	<div class="view">
	  <p>
		  In this example both resources have a clear owner. Try-with-resources closes the Mailer first and the debug stream afterwards, so Jakarta Mail
		  cannot write to an already closed stream.
	  </p>
	</div>

	<div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (PrintStream jakartaMailDebug = new PrintStream(
        Files.newOutputStream(Paths.get("jakarta-mail-debug.log")));
     Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("host", 587, "username", "password")
        .withDebugLogging(true)
        .withDebugPrinter(jakartaMailDebug)
        .buildMailer()) {

    mailer.sync().testConnection();
}
</code></pre>
	</div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-smtp-capabilities" id="section-smtp-capabilities" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Inspect SMTP capabilities and TLS</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        A connection works on your laptop but behaves differently in production. Did STARTTLS happen? Does that endpoint advertise DSN?
        Did authentication actually succeed? <code>mailer.sync().probeConnection()</code> opens a fresh connection and returns those facts in an
        immutable <code>SmtpConnectionReport</code>. It sends no email and does not borrow a connection from your send pool.
      </p>
      <p>
        The CLI exposes the same report through <code>sjm probe</code>, with an optional <code>--authenticate</code> flag.
        See the <a href="cli.html#section-probe">CLI examples and exit codes</a> for one-shot and daemon use.
      </p>
      <p>
        The default probe does not authenticate with the SMTP server: it neither consults cached SMTP credentials nor calls your Jakarta Mail
        authenticator or OAuth2 token provider. Configured proxy authentication and TLS client certificates still apply.
        Pass <code>true</code> to test the configured credentials as well. This choice applies only to this probe; ordinary sending is unchanged.
        Authentication follows your existing TLS and AUTH settings, so check those before enabling it against an unfamiliar endpoint.
      </p>
      <p>
        The synchronous methods always run on the caller's thread. <code>mailer.async().probeConnection()</code> and
        <code>mailer.async().probeConnection(true)</code> use the Mailer's executor and complete after transport and proxy cleanup have been attempted.
        Execution mode is selected by the view, not by configuration. Connection failures become reports; scheduling and Mailer-shutdown failures
        complete an async future exceptionally.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">try (Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()
        .mailerBuilder()
        .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.org", 587)
        .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
        .buildMailer()) {
    SmtpConnectionReport report = mailer.sync().probeConnection();
    logger.info("SMTP connection:\n{}", report);

    report.getEffectiveCapabilities().ifPresent(capabilities -&gt; {
        logger.info("DSN advertised: {}", capabilities.supports("DSN"));
        capabilities.getMaximumMessageSize().ifPresent(bytes -&gt;
            logger.info("Advertised SIZE limit: {} bytes", bytes));
    });

    report.getFailurePhase().ifPresent(phase -&gt;
        logger.warn("{}: {}", phase,
            report.getFailureDescription().orElse("No detail available")));
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-java small">// On a Mailer with credentials already configured:
SmtpConnectionReport withAuthentication = mailer.sync().probeConnection(true);
boolean authenticated = withAuthentication.isAuthenticated();

// Or run the probe in the background; keep the Mailer open until it finishes.
CompletableFuture&lt;SmtpConnectionReport&gt; pending =
    mailer.async().probeConnection();</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h3>Compare SMTP features before and after STARTTLS</h3>
      <p>
        This illustrative report shows a server that advertises a different SIZE limit and adds DSN after STARTTLS.
        <code>getBeforeTls()</code> is history; <code>getAfterTls()</code> describes the encrypted EHLO. Use
        <code>getEffectiveCapabilities()</code> when you want the applicable snapshot. Failed connection setup, rejected post-TLS EHLO,
        HELO-only fallback, and oversized diagnostic replies can leave it empty. Empty means unknown, not &ldquo;no extensions supported.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>getExtensions()</code> includes unknown extensions and repeated advertisements, in sorted name order.
        <code>supports("DSN")</code> is case-insensitive; the same lookup works for SMTPUTF8, 8BITMIME, PIPELINING, CHUNKING,
        BINARYMIME and REQUIRETLS. These are server advertisements, not claims that Simple Java Mail used or implemented those features.
        In particular, this probe adds no PIPELINING or CHUNKING send path.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>getMaximumMessageSize()</code> only returns a consistent positive numeric SIZE limit. An absent limit, zero (no fixed maximum),
        conflicting duplicates and malformed values return empty. Check <code>supports("SIZE")</code> separately.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-text small">SMTP connection probe: SUCCESS
Endpoint: smtp.example.org:587 (smtp)
Greeting: 220 smtp.example.org ready
Before TLS: {SIZE=[10485760], STARTTLS=[]}
STARTTLS: completed
After TLS: {AUTH=[LOGIN PLAIN], DSN=[], SIZE=[26214400]}
TLS active during setup: true
TLS details: TLSv1.3, cipher=TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384, peer=[CN=smtp.example.org, smtp.example.org], configured identity check=true, custom hostname verifier=not observed
Authentication: not requested
Elapsed: 138ms</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <h3>Keep configuration, negotiation and delivery apart</h3>
      <p>
        Read the configured transport strategy from <code>mailer.getTransportStrategy()</code>. The probe reports what happened
        during connection setup; it does not repeat that configuration setting.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>getTlsDetails()</code> exposes the negotiated TLS protocol, cipher and public certificate identities when Angus's supported hooks
        allow it. The identity-check flag is configured policy, not a new trust verdict. A custom hostname verifier's result is separate,
        and handshake metadata can exist even when a later verifier rejects the connection. A verifier configured by class name is left
        untouched; TLS details are unavailable in that case. Trust, hostname checks and caller-supplied socket factories are not weakened
        to collect diagnostics.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Angus probe reports at most 32 certificate identities, including the certificate subject. If there are more, the report warns
        that additional names were omitted. This limit keeps diagnostic output manageable; it does not affect certificate verification.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>isSuccessful()</code> includes connection setup, requested authentication and cleanup. <code>isConnected()</code> means setup
        completed, not that a socket remains open. Inspect the failure phase and earlier snapshots when a probe fails; raw provider
        exceptions and AUTH exchanges are not stored. Display text is bounded and control characters are escaped for logs, though the
        report intentionally contains endpoint and public server/certificate names.
      </p>
      <p>
        The probe uses the Mailer's existing endpoint, proxy, TLS settings and provider timeouts. It explicitly connects even in logging-only
        mode. The total <em>mail-send</em> deadline does not apply, and cancelling the returned future does not abort network I/O.
        Timing starts when execution begins, so it excludes executor queue time. A pooled Mailer probes its configured endpoint, not every
        cluster member, and it does not call the mail-send observer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Angus SMTP/SMTPS is supported through a dedicated probe adapter. Another provider needs its own
        <code>SmtpConnectionProbeAdapter</code>; absent support or a <code>CustomMailer</code> produces an explicit unsupported report without
        opening a connection. Your original Session's properties and provider registration are not rewritten.
        An adapter can confirm a successful connection without exposing the full EHLO reply or TLS session details. Those fields stay
        unavailable; that does not mean the connection failed or the server advertised no extensions.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-text small">SMTP connection probe: FAILED
Endpoint: smtp.example.org:587 (smtp)
Greeting: 220 smtp.example.org ready
Before TLS: {SIZE=[10485760]}
STARTTLS: not attempted
After TLS: not available
TLS active during setup: false
TLS details: not available
Authentication: not requested
Failure at STARTTLS: STARTTLS did not complete. Check whether the server offers TLS, its certificate and hostname, and your trust settings.
Elapsed: 42ms</code></pre>
      <p>This illustrative failure retains the plain EHLO, but does not present it as usable capabilities after the required TLS setup failed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wide">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Use</th><th>Connects to SMTP?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Can the normal connection path open?</td><td><code>mailer.sync().testConnection()</code> or its async equivalent</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What did this endpoint advertise and negotiate?</td><td><code>mailer.sync().probeConnection()</code> or its async equivalent</td><td>Yes, on a fresh dedicated connection</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Will this email pass preparation?</td><td><code>validate(email)</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What bytes and envelope will preparation produce?</td><td><code>rehearse(email)</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Where did a loaded setting come from?</td><td><a href="#section-config-diagnostics"><code>mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code></a></td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happened to an actual send?</td><td><a href="/analyzing-send-results.html">Submission receipt and mail-send outcome</a></td><td>The send does</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        A successful probe proves neither future message acceptance nor inbox delivery. Capabilities may change between connections and
        endpoints. Run the manual
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/codex/10.0.0/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/SmtpConnectionProbeDemoApp.java">SmtpConnectionProbeDemoApp</a>
        for a complete example: without arguments it starts a loopback-only SMTP peer, prints a real report and sends no email.
        Its <code>--configured</code> mode instead uses the shared demo SMTP settings; add <code>--authenticate</code> only to that mode to test credentials.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-config-diagnostics" id="section-config-diagnostics" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Trace loaded configuration values</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        When a factory picks up an unexpected SMTP host, timeout, proxy, or email default, inspect the configuration snapshot it captured. The diagnostic
        report shows every configured value that won and the source that supplied it. Passwords, keys, certificates, and suspicious extra Jakarta Mail
        properties are shown as <code class="inline language-text">&lt;redacted&gt;</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        With Spring, the source is the underlying Spring <code class="inline language-java">PropertySource</code>, such as a profile-specific properties
        file, system properties, or environment variables. Spring placeholders are resolved normally, while the report names the source that declared the
        <code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.*</code> property. See the
        <a href="/configuration.html#section-config-properties">configuration precedence</a> and
        <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-profiles">Spring profile</a> guides for the corresponding loading rules.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();

LOGGER.info(
    "Resolved Simple Java Mail configuration:\n{}",
    mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics());
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        <code class="inline language-java">mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> describes the immutable factory snapshot used by both Email and Mailer
        builders. It does not include values supplied later to a builder, generated runtime defaults, settings hidden inside a caller-owned Jakarta Mail
        <code class="inline language-java">Session</code>, or live pool and transport state.
      </p>
      <p>
        After building a Mailer, use <code class="inline language-java">mailer.getOperationalConfig()</code> for its effective operational settings. Use
        the factory diagnostics when the question is where a property-backed value came from. The regular
        <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailConfig</code> getters and <code class="inline language-java">asMap()</code> return real values,
        including credentials, so do not log those directly.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">ConfigDiagnostics diagnostics =
    mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics();

for (ConfigDiagnosticGroup group : diagnostics.getGroups()) {
    System.out.println(group.getDisplayName());

    for (ConfigPropertyDiagnostic property
            : diagnostics.getProperties(group)) {
        System.out.printf(
            "  %s = %s (source: %s)%n",
            property.getPropertyName(),
            property.getDisplayValue(),
            property.getSourceName());
    }
}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-config-diagnostics-demo" id="section-config-diagnostics-demo" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Mixed-source example</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        The runnable
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/develop/modules/simple-java-mail/src/test/java/demo/ConfigDiagnosticsDemoApp.java">ConfigDiagnosticsDemoApp</a>
        builds a realistic source hierarchy from fake, in-memory properties and prints the resulting report. It does not build a Mailer or connect to an
        SMTP server. The output shown here is abridged; each <code class="inline language-text">...</code> only replaces more entries from the demo.
      </p>
      <ul class="indent">
        <li>
          The SMTP host comes from the profile-specific file, while the SMTP port and cluster maximum come from command-line arguments. Winners are tracked
          per property, not per group.
        </li>
        <li>
          Values such as <code class="inline language-text">02525</code> and <code class="inline language-text">01</code> are shown as the parsed numbers
          <code class="inline language-text">2525</code> and <code class="inline language-text">1</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Passwords, private keys, and the OAuth token are redacted. The ordinary <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.auth</code> property
          stays visible.
        </li>
        <li>
          A final source supplies blank SMTP username and proxy host values, so the earlier non-blank values remain. Control characters in the remote source
          name and default subject are escaped for logging.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <strong>Abridged demo output</strong>
<pre><code class="language-text small">SMTP connection:
