    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-async-queue" id="section-async-queue" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Inspect async queue pressure</h2>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        When background mail falls behind, <code>mailer.getAsyncQueueSnapshot()</code> shows waiting tasks, active workers, configured limits,
        shutdown state, and cumulative rejection counts. It contains no message content. Counts are instantaneous estimates, not a promise that a
        later send will fit. Take a new snapshot to see new activity; previous snapshots do not change.
      </p>
      <p>
        A worker waiting for a connection-pool lease is active, and an async simple batch counts as one task. Async connection tests share these counts.
        For a caller-owned executor the result is empty: use that executor's own diagnostics.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is changing runtime activity. <code>mailer.getOperationalConfig().getAsyncQueueConfig()</code> describes settings, while
        <code>mail.getConfig().getDiagnostics()</code> traces loaded property defaults. The
        <a href="configuration.html#section-mail-send-observer">mail-send observer</a> reports each attempt's queue delay through
        <code>readyAt</code> and <code>startedAt</code>. See <a href="sending-and-execution.html#section-async-queue">queue configuration and overflow policies</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java small">mailer.getAsyncQueueSnapshot().ifPresent(queue -&gt; {
    logger.info("Mail queue: {}/{}, active workers: {}/{}, closing: {}",
        queue.getQueuedCount(), queue.getConfiguration().getCapacity(),
        queue.getActiveCount(), queue.getWorkerLimit(), queue.isShutdown());
    logger.info("Rejected operations: {}", queue.getRejectionCounts());
});</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-text small">Mail queue: 100/100, active workers: 4/4, closing: false
Rejected operations: {QUEUE_FULL=12, CAPACITY_WAIT_TIMED_OUT=0,
    CAPACITY_WAIT_INTERRUPTED=0, EXECUTOR_SHUT_DOWN=0}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-inspect-mailer-email" id="section-inspect-mailer-email" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Inspect a mailer or an email</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        After building a Mailer or an Email instance, you can read everything back from it and see if it is what you expected.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can verify default values this way as well.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>email.getAnythingYouCanSetWithABuilder();

mailer.getSession();
mailer.getTransportStrategy();
mailer.getServerConfig();
mailer.getProxyConfig();
mailer.getOperationalConfig();
// default S/MIME signing, validation criteria etc.
mailer.getEmailGovernance();
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>


  <a href="#section-mailexception" id="section-mailexception" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Catching exceptions</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        During a synchronous send, Simple Java Mail throws a <code class="inline language-java">MailException</code> if preparation, validation or sending
        fails. This includes checked exceptions translated from the underlying frameworks.
      </p>
      <p>
        An asynchronous send reports those failures through its <code class="inline language-java">CompletableFuture</code>. See
        <a href="features.html#section-handling-async-result">Handling asynchronous results</a> for an example. Choose
        <code class="inline language-java">mailer.sync().sendMail(email)</code> for direct exceptions, or the async view for completion-based handling.
      </p>
      <p>
        A failed send does not always mean nothing was submitted. See <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html">Analyzing send results</a> for a
        recipient-by-recipient example, the receipt and server-response APIs, and how to decide whether retrying could send someone a duplicate.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="side">
<pre><code>
try {
   mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
} catch (MailException e) {
   // handle the exception
}
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-logging" id="section-logging" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
  <h2>Logging output</h2>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Simple Java Mail uses <a href="https://www.slf4j.org/">SLF4J</a> to log, which means you can use any logging framework you like that supports
        it. Also read this excellent summary of <a href="https://learnjava-soa.blogspot.com/2015/06/slf4j-with-log4j2-example.html">how to configure
                                                                                                                                  slf4j with
                                                                                                                                  log4j2</a>.
      </p>
      <div>
        Aside from the native Jakarta Mail logging, the following parts of Simple Java Mail may produce additional logging:
        <ul class="indent">
          <li><strong>"org.simplejavamail"</strong></li>
          <li><strong>"org.simplejavamail.internal.clisupport"</strong></li>
          <li><strong>"org.simplejavamail.internal.dkimsupport"</strong></li>
          <li><strong>"org.simplejavamail.internal.smimesupport"</strong></li>
          <li><strong>"org.simplejavamail.internal.authenticatedsockssupport"</strong></li>
          <li><strong>"socks5bridge"</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>The latter two are for the proxy bridge that is used for
          <a href="features.html#section-proxy">authenticated proxy connections</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        If you wish to know where and how the properties are loaded, you can increase logging for <strong>"org.simplejavamail.config.ConfigLoader"</strong>.
        This will tell you which properties are loaded by API and which from config file and which properties are without value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Simple Java Mail logs through SLF4J, leaving the logging backend and its configuration to your application. If you use Log4j 2,
        <a href="https://github.com/bbottema/simple-java-mail/blob/develop/modules/simple-java-mail/src/main/resources/log4j2_example.xml"><code class="inline language-text">log4j2_example.xml</code></a>
        provides a starting point that you can copy and adapt. More examples follow below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-log4j2" id="section-log4j2" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Example with Log4j 2</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Add the matching SLF4J binding for <a href="https://logging.apache.org/log4j/2.x/">Log4j 2</a>, then place this configuration in
        <code class="inline language-text">log4j2.xml</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-markup small">&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;configuration status=&quot;OFF&quot;&gt;
    &lt;appenders&gt;
        &lt;Console name=&quot;console&quot; target=&quot;SYSTEM_OUT&quot;&gt;
            &lt;PatternLayout pattern=&quot;%d{HH:mm:ss} [%t] %-5level %c{1} - %msg%n&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/Console&gt;
        &lt;Console name=&quot;simpleConsole&quot; target=&quot;SYSTEM_OUT&quot;&gt;
            &lt;PatternLayout pattern=&quot;%d Simple Java Mail SOCKS5 bridge - %level %m%n&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/Console&gt;
    &lt;/appenders&gt;
    &lt;Loggers&gt;
        &lt;Logger name=&quot;org.simplejavamail&quot; level=&quot;trace&quot;/&gt;
        &lt;!-- in case you&#39;re using authenticated proxy --&gt;
        &lt;Logger name=&quot;socks5bridge&quot; level=&quot;info&quot; additivity=&quot;false&quot;&gt;
            &lt;AppenderRef ref=&quot;simpleConsole&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/Logger&gt;
        &lt;Logger name=&quot;org.simplejavamail.internal.authenticatedsockssupport&quot; level=&quot;warn&quot;/&gt;

        &lt;Root level=&quot;warn&quot;&gt;
            &lt;AppenderRef ref=&quot;console&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/Root&gt;
    &lt;/Loggers&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#section-logback" id="section-logback" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Example with Logback</h3>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        Add <a href="https://logback.qos.ch/">Logback</a> to the classpath, then place this configuration in
        <code class="inline language-text">logback.xml</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="wide full-width-example">
<pre><code class="language-markup small">&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;UTF-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;configuration&gt;
    &lt;appender name=&quot;console&quot; class=&quot;ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender&quot;&gt;
        &lt;encoder&gt;
            &lt;pattern&gt;%d{HH:mm:ss.SSS} [%thread] %-5level %c{1} - %msg%n&lt;/pattern&gt;
        &lt;/encoder&gt;
    &lt;/appender&gt;
    &lt;appender name=&quot;simpleConsole&quot; class=&quot;ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender&quot;&gt;
        &lt;encoder&gt;
            &lt;pattern&gt;%d Simple Java Mail SOCKS5 bridge - %level %m%n&lt;/pattern&gt;
        &lt;/encoder&gt;
    &lt;/appender&gt;

    &lt;logger name=&quot;org.simplejavamail&quot; level=&quot;TRACE&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;!-- in case you&#39;re using authenticated proxy --&gt;
    &lt;logger name=&quot;socks5bridge&quot; level=&quot;INFO&quot; additivity=&quot;false&quot;&gt;
        &lt;appender-ref ref=&quot;simpleConsole&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/logger&gt;
    &lt;logger name=&quot;org.simplejavamail.internal.authenticatedsockssupport&quot; level=&quot;WARN&quot;/&gt;

    &lt;root level=&quot;WARN&quot;&gt;
        &lt;appender-ref ref=&quot;console&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/root&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;
</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>
